# The annotations of the turbo loader $F000-$F0FF (tape block 4): the source of src/turbo/turbo.asm and
# src/turbo/turbo/loader.asm. Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md. Witness: c1 (work/stage1/c1, power-on, the ROM
# blocks through the fast LD-BYTES trap, block 5 at signal speed); counts from python3 tools/l1sum.py --builds stage1/c1.
@equ $5C3D ErrSp 2 ; ERR_SP, the address of the BASIC error return on the machine stack
@equ $053F SaLdRet ; the ROM's SA/LD-RET: restores the border, tests BREAK, EI, RET
@equ $05EC LdEdgeTail ; the ROM's LD-EDGE-1 after its delay loop: AND A, then LD-SAMPLE $05ED (the edge loop)
@equ $8000 GameEntry ; the game's first instruction (block 5's first byte)

@module loader ; The turbo loader: a copy of the ROM's LD-BYTES with its own timings, a 16-bit sum, the jump to the game
@about BASIC line 30 (RANDOMIZE USR 61440) enters TurboStart. It loads the 25000 bytes of tape block 5 to $8000-$E1A7 with TurboLdBytes, a byte-for-byte copy of the ROM's LD-BYTES $0556 with three timing constants changed (the turbo block's zero is 660 T and its one 1320 T where the ROM's are 855 T and 1710 T), adds the bytes into a 16-bit sum, compares it with GameSum and jumps to the game at $8000.
@about A load error or a wrong sum jumps to 0, a reset: TurboStart first writes 0 into the ERR_SP target, so the ROM's error return would reset too. c1: entered at frame 250 (T 15 109), the block loads from frame 300 to 5529, the game is entered at frame 5556 (T 25 555).

$F000 c TurboStart ; Load the game, check its sum, run it
> Entered by BASIC line 30 (RANDOMIZE USR 61440, c1 frame 250, T 15 109; one entry). Writes 0 into the two bytes ERR_SP points at (the address on the machine stack that a BASIC error returns to), so an error report would jump to 0 and reset the machine.
> Then TurboLdBytes with IX = $8000, DE = $61A8 (25000), A = $FF (the flag of a data block), carry set (load, not verify). No carry back is a load error: JP 0. Then TurboSumLoop adds the 25000 bytes into HL (each byte as BC = 0..255), compares HL with GameSum ($2E3F) and jumps to 0 when they differ, to the game at $8000 when they agree (c1 frame 5556, T 25 555).
> Outputs: none (it never returns). The sum is of the data bytes only; the XOR checksum of the tape block is TurboLdBytes' own check (TurboLoadDone).
$F015 ; no carry: the load failed (BREAK, a bad flag, the XOR check): reset
$F022 TurboSumLoop ; HL += the byte at IX, 25000 times (c1: 25000 passes)
$F033 ; HL - GameSum: not zero is a wrong sum, reset
$F039 ; enter the game

$F03C w GameSum ; The 16-bit sum of the 25000 bytes of block 5: $2E3F
> Read once by TurboStart (LD BC,(GameSum) at $F02F, c1). tools/image.py asserts the same sum on the tape's bytes.

$F03E c TurboLdBytes ; LD-BYTES with the turbo timings: load DE bytes at IX, flag A
> A copy of the ROM's LD-BYTES $0556-$05E2 instruction for instruction, with three constants changed: the timing counter B starts at $C4 (ROM $B0) before the marker and each later bit, at $C6 (ROM $B2) before the first bit of each byte, and a bit is a one when B passes $D5 (ROM $CB). The edge loop itself is the ROM's: TurboEdge1 delays and jumps to the ROM at $05EC, which counts B up by one every 59 T until the EAR bit changes. Inputs: IX the destination, DE the length, A the flag, carry set for LOAD (clear would verify). Output: carry set when the flag, every byte and the XOR checksum were right.
> Like the ROM's it pushes SaLdRet ($053F) as its return: every exit goes through the ROM's SA/LD-RET (border back, BREAK test, EI). It saves A and the carry in AF' (INC D / EX AF,AF' / DEC D), sets DI and the border to 15 (OUT $FE, $0F), and keeps in C the last EAR level with the border colour in bits 0-2 (red/cyan stripes on the pilot, blue/yellow on the data after the XOR $03).
> c1: one entry at frame 250, the block's last byte at frame 5529 (T 63 143); TurboByteLoop 25001 passes (the flag and 25000 bytes; the checksum byte ends the loop), TurboBitLoop 200016.
$F053 TurboBreak ; RET NZ: BREAK pressed or the edge timed out (to SaLdRet)
$F054 TurboStartSearch ; wait for the first edge, then a 1 s pause (DJNZ over HL = $0415) and another edge
$F05C TurboPause ; 1045 x 256 DJNZ passes, about a second (c1: 267 285 passes)
$F068 TurboLeader ; 256 pilot pulses each of B from $9C to more than $C6
$F077 TurboSync ; wait for the short sync pulse (B below $D4), then its second edge
$F091 TurboByteLoop ; one byte: the first is the flag (TurboFlag), the rest stored at IX (or verified)
$F09B TurboFlag ; the flag byte: wrong flag returns NZ; the byte loop counts one more for it (INC DE)
$F0A5 TurboVerify ; VERIFY: compare with the byte at IX (not executed in c1, carry was set)
$F0AA TurboNext ; IX + 1
$F0AC TurboCount ; DE - 1, timing constant $C6 for the first bit
$F0B0 TurboMarker ; L = 1: the marker bit, shifted out after 8 bits
$F0B2 TurboBitLoop ; one bit: two edges, B compared with $D5 (one when it passed it), counter $C4 for the next
$F0C0 TurboByteDone ; H ^= L: the XOR checksum; DE = 0 ends the block
$F0C7 TurboLoadDone ; carry set when the XOR of the flag, the bytes and the checksum byte is 0 (H = 0, CP 1)

$F0CB c TurboEdge2 ; Two edges: TurboEdge1 twice (the ROM's LD-EDGE-2)
> Called 200 273 times in c1 (frames 300-5529): one per bit, one per pilot pulse and the sync's first half. Output as TurboEdge1; NC after the first edge returns NC.

$F0CF c TurboEdge1 ; One edge: the ROM's delay (A = $16) then the ROM's own edge loop at $05EC
> The ROM's LD-EDGE-1 $05E7 has the same delay; the loader keeps its own copy and jumps into the ROM after it, at $05EC (AND A, then LD-SAMPLE $05ED), so the ROM's edge loop counts B (INC B every pass, RET Z at the wrap = no edge), reads EAR, flips the border, and returns carry set with B the count. c1: 401 976 calls (frames 250-5529).

$F0D1 TurboDelay ; 22 passes of DEC A
$F0D7 b TurboLeftover 10 ; Bytes after the code no instruction reads: 10 bytes, then zeros, then "*TES*RESU*"
> No recording reads or runs $F0D7-$F0FF (c1: only the load writes them). 10 bytes ($2B $24 $05 $00 $29 $40 $03 $00 $28 $21), 18 zeros, the text "*TES*RESU*" (USER and SET backwards), 3 zeros: what the author's development system left in the 256-byte block. Kept as bytes.
$F0E1 ; zeros
$F0F3 ; "*TES*RESU*"
$F0FD ; zeros
